<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Leiningen</h1><p class="st-meta">1128 CE – 1806 CE (approx.) · capital Dürkheim</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Palatinate county ruled by the Leiningen family from the Haardt hills, raised to a principality in 1779 before being swept away by the French.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Count Emich I of Leiningen appears around 1128, with castles in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haardt">Haardt</a> hills of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Palatinate</a>. The original line died out in 1220, and the lands passed through a sister to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saarbrucken">Saarbrücken</a> family, who took the Leiningen name. The family split in 1317 into Leiningen-Hardenburg and Leiningen-Dagsburg.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts ruled scattered territories in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Palatinate</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>, with castles at Hardenburg and Altleiningen. They were often in rivalry with the Electoral Palatinate. The Hardenburg line moved its residence to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-durkheim">Dürkheim</a> in the 18th century and was raised to princely rank in 1779.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>French revolutionary forces annexed the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in the 1790s. In 1803 the princes received former church lands at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amorbach">Amorbach</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a>, but these were mediatised in 1806 and divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria's</a> half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-3rd-prince-of-leiningen">Carl</a> was a prince of Leiningen.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-leiningen.jpg" alt="Principality of Leiningen" width="320" height="291"><figcaption>Image: Frank · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fuerstentum_Leiningen.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1128 CE – 1806 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dürkheim</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Emich I of Leiningen, Karl Friedrich Wilhelm of Leiningen</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity, later Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Rhine Franconian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>County of Leiningen, Leiningen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1467&amp;focus=principality-of-leiningen" aria-label="Principality of Leiningen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4892 449 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Leiningen lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5212 705L5229 694M5212 705L5194 723"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5194 723h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5212 705h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5212 705h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1467&amp;focus=principality-of-leiningen">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
